			"130": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 2: WRITING TO INDICES\n\nIn this example we use the Sequence node together with the Write node to rewrite part of the collection.\nThe Sequence node is great for creating a list of indices as you can decide where to start(begin), what the inteverval between indices is(step) and how big the collection should be(size).\n\nWe start with a single Saw oscillator that we Expand to 10 instances.\nNext we take a Sequence node and generate the collection [ 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ].\nThe Write node is used to write the value coming from the Sine node to the indices\/positions generated by the Sequence node.\nThe result is that the Saw values on position 3, 4, 5 and 6 arebeing overwritten by the Sine value.\nThis is why the 4 dots in the centre are moving up and down(sine) and the outside dots jump back to the start(saw).\n\nThe same concept is also applied to the colors.\nWe take a white color, expand it to 10 and write the red color to position 3, 4, 5 and 6."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 945.18505859375,
					"width": 1742.626708984375,
					"x": -134.336029052734375,
					"y": 738.353515625
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ff20c7bb",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
